  <h1>How often should architects change their Facebook ads?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every marketing blog has a number for this. Change your creative every two weeks, every
    month, whenever frequency hits three. We went and looked at what actually happened to 418
    ads run for architecture firms, and the calendar turned out to be the least useful thing to
    watch.
  </p>

  <p>
    We run Meta advertising for architecture firms and we can read every ad's results day by day. Between 28 March and 16 October 2026, 20 US architecture and design firm accounts ran 418 ads that spent $104,404 and brought in 2,307 lead form submissions. Every figure below comes from that set, counted per ad, per day.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Change an ad when its results say so. In our data, an ad with no lead after its first $100 never turned into a good one, so cut it there. A winning ad can run for months: our 21 longest-running ads lost only about 12 percent of their efficiency after week eight. Keep three to five ads live and add a few new ones each month, because in a typical account one ad ends up producing half of all the leads.</p>
  </div>

  <p>
    This page is the creative side of <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>. That page covers what to put in the ad. This one covers how long to leave it there, and it sits inside the wider system in <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One ad typically carries half your leads</h2>
  <p>
    Start here, because it changes how you think about every other number. In the 19 accounts with at least 20 leads, the single best ad produced 50 percent of the account's leads at the median. The best three produced 83 percent.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What we measured (per account, 19 accounts)</th><th>Median</th><th>Range</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Share of leads from the best ad</td><td>50%</td><td>23% to 95%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Share of leads from the best three ads</td><td>83%</td><td>53% to 100%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Days the best ad ran</td><td>35</td><td>10 to 130</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ads live on a typical day</td><td>4</td><td>3 to 5 (middle half)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New ads launched per month</td><td>6</td><td>3 to 9 (middle half)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    So the job is mostly finding the next winner and protecting the current one. Which ad wins is hard to call in advance. We have been doing this a long time and still get surprised by which photo takes off, which is the honest reason to keep testing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same shape shows up one level up. In our <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#one-campaign-does-most-of-the-work">benchmark report</a>, one campaign produced 84 percent of a typical account's leads. Ads and campaigns both concentrate hard on a winner.
  </p>

  <h2>How long a Facebook ad lasts before it stops working</h2>
  <p>
    Longer than the blogs suggest, when it is a good one. We took the 21 ads that ran for eight weeks or more and lined up their own results by age.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Ad age</th><th>Spend</th><th>Leads</th><th>Cost per lead</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Week 1</td><td>$2,306</td><td>39</td><td>$59</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Week 2</td><td>$1,584</td><td>28</td><td>$57</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weeks 3 to 4</td><td>$3,027</td><td>56</td><td>$54</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weeks 5 to 8</td><td>$5,558</td><td>97</td><td>$57</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Week 9 and later</td><td>$7,953</td><td>125</td><td>$64</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Over the first eight weeks these ads ran at $56.70 per lead. After week eight, $63.62. That is a 12 percent drift over two months and more, and the link click rate stayed between 1.6 and 1.9 percent the whole time. People kept clicking at the same rate. They just converted a touch less.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fair caveat: these 21 are survivors. They ran long because they were working, so they say what a good ad does over time, not what an average ad does. That is still the useful question. Nobody agonizes over when to retire a bad ad.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also checked whether ads looked worse as they aged once you compare each one against the other ads running in the same account the same week, which strips out the seasons. They didn't, in any clean way. Our earlier finding that <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#why-did-my-cost-per-lead-go-up-after-a-few-months">cost per lead mostly moves with the season</a> holds at the ad level too. If your cost per lead jumps in April, the ad is probably not the problem.
  </p>

  <h2>The $100 rule for cutting an ad</h2>
  <p>
    This is the most practical number on the page. We followed every ad from its first dollar and asked one question: had it produced a lead yet?
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Checked after</th><th>Ads with no lead yet</th><th>Cost per lead afterwards, ads with no lead</th><th>Cost per lead afterwards, ads with a lead</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>$50 spent</td><td>40% (85 of 211)</td><td>$113</td><td>$37</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$100 spent</td><td>21% (31 of 148)</td><td>$156</td><td>$40</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$150 spent</td><td>10% (12 of 117)</td><td>$113</td><td>$41</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    At $50 it is too early. Four in ten ads have nothing yet, and some of those come good. By $100 the picture has mostly settled: an ad with zero leads at that point went on to cost about four times as much per lead as one that had landed even a single lead. Of the ones that kept running, not one went on to beat $60 per lead.
  </p>
  <p>
    The underlying logic is simple. Our accounts paid around $45 per form lead overall, so $100 is roughly two leads' worth of spend. An ad that can't find one person in that budget is telling you something. If your own cost per lead runs much higher or lower, scale the rule to about two of your leads.
  </p>
  <p>
    Testing this way is cheap. Of the 234 ads that spent at least $50, 52 never produced a lead at all. Together they took 5.3 percent of total spend. That is the price of finding the winners, and it is a small one.
  </p>

  <h2>Why architecture ads don't follow the e-commerce rules</h2>
  <p>
    A lot of the creative refresh advice online is written with big e-commerce budgets in mind. Meta's own help center says an ad set is <a href="https://www.facebook.com/business/help/269269737396981" rel="nofollow">learning limited</a> when it is unlikely to get around 50 optimization events in a week. A typical architecture account in our benchmark gets 22 leads in a whole month. You will never hit 50 a week on one ad set, and you don't need to.
  </p>
  <p>
    What that means for you: the platform's automatic signals have very little data to work with on a budget like yours. Meta describes <a href="https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1346816142327858" rel="nofollow">creative fatigue</a> as an audience seeing the same creative too many times, which can push the cost per result up. At a few leads a week, you will usually spot that in your own cost per lead before any recommendation shows up in Ads Manager.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look at cost per lead over two to four weeks, and leave the daily numbers alone. A single day without a lead is normal: in the benchmark, 40 percent of live account days had zero leads.
  </p>

  <h2>Residential and commercial ads wear out differently</h2>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, the pattern is the same as everywhere else. Across seven residential accounts the best ad carried 50 percent of leads at the median and 21 percent of ads that spent $50 or more never produced a lead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your audience is large, since plenty of people in a metro are thinking about a house, so a strong ad can run a long time before the same people see it too often. Refreshing usually means a new project photo, a different room, or the same house at a different time of day.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, your audience is smaller by nature. There are only so many developers, business owners and property managers in one metro, so the same people see the same ad sooner. We only have two commercial accounts in this set, too few to measure a separate lifespan, so this part is reasoning and not a benchmark.
  </p>
  <p>
    A small audience means you should expect fatigue sooner and keep more variety in rotation: finished buildings, a cost or schedule outcome, and a short line about the kind of project you take on. A developer comparing firms also needs several pieces of proof, so a rotation does double duty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both segments find their architect through the same feed, which is why the playbooks in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a> and <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a> both lean on paid social, with different creative.
  </p>

  <h2>A simple routine for changing your ads</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Keep three to five ads live.</strong> That was the typical count in our accounts, and it is enough to always have a challenger running next to the winner.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut any ad with no lead after about two leads' worth of spend.</strong> For most of our accounts that is $100.</li>
    <li><strong>Leave winners alone.</strong> If the cost per lead over the last two to four weeks is holding, don't touch it. Our best ads ran a median of five weeks, and the longest ran 130 days.</li>
    <li><strong>Add one or two new ads every week or two.</strong> Our accounts averaged about six new ads a month. Most will lose, and that is fine.</li>
    <li><strong>Retire a winner when its cost per lead has risen by a third or more for a few weeks</strong> and a newer ad is beating it. Rising cost alone in spring is often the season.</li>
    <li><strong>Make new ads from your own work.</strong> A fresh photo of a finished project is the cheapest new creative you have. What tends to work in the creative itself is covered in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects#the-creative-that-works-and-the-kind-that-quietly-flops">the creative that works and the kind that quietly flops</a>, and video gets its own answer in <a href="/video-marketing-for-architects#do-video-ads-work-better-than-photos-on-facebook">video marketing for architects</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What this data can't tell you</h2>
  <p>
    Honest limits. We count lead form submissions, which is where most leads come in on these accounts (78 percent in the benchmark), so ads sending people to a landing page are judged a little harshly here. We don't have reach and frequency at the ad level, so we can't tell you the frequency at which an architecture ad starts to tire.
  </p>
  <p>
    We can't see why each ad was switched off: some were replaced by a newer winner, some accounts paused for a season. And six and a half months is long enough to measure ad lifespans but short of a full year.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also can't publish a clean comparison of image against video ads yet. The only format label in the data is the ad's name, and too many ads have generic names to trust the split.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>How often should you change your Facebook ads?</h3>
  <p>
    When the numbers say so. Across 418 ads at 20 US architecture firms, good ads held their cost per lead for two months or more (a 12 percent rise after week eight), while not one ad that was still leadless after its first $100 went on to beat $60 per lead. The practical routine: cut losers at about two leads' worth of spend, leave winners running while their cost holds, and add one or two new ads every week or two so a replacement is always being tested.
  </p>
  <h3>How long does a Facebook ad last for an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    The best ad in each of our accounts ran a median of 35 days, and the longest ran 130. Across all ads that spent at least $50, the median ran about 15 days, mostly because weak ads get switched off early. A strong ad aimed at a large residential audience can run for months. A commercial audience is smaller, so expect the same people to see your ad sooner and plan to rotate more often.
  </p>
  <h3>How do you know if your Facebook ad has creative fatigue?</h3>
  <p>
    Your cost per lead climbs for several weeks in a row while your other ads hold steady. Compare the ad against the rest of your account in the same weeks, because a rise that hits every ad at once is usually the season and not fatigue. At a few leads a week, judge on two to four weeks of results, never on one quiet day.
  </p>
  <h3>How many Facebook ads should an architecture firm run at once?</h3>
  <p>
    Three to five is typical in our accounts, with a median of four live on any given day. That is enough to always have one or two challengers running beside your winner without spreading a modest budget so thin that no single ad gets enough spend to prove itself. Our <a href="/architecture-marketing-budget-calculator">architecture marketing budget calculator</a> shows what a given budget usually buys.
  </p>

  <h2>Let the winners run</h2>
  <p>
    The two usual ways to get this wrong are changing too much too early and never testing anything new. The fix is boring and it works: a few ads live, losers cut at a set amount, winners left alone, and a steady trickle of fresh project photos.
  </p>
